Trace:

  1. unstakeAndExitPool runs in the library, but it is reached by _delegateCall(LP_LIB, ...) from the vault (same
  pattern as _transferYieldTokenToOwner), so all exit proceeds — ERC20 or native — land in the vault's own
  storage/balance context. Nothing is "left behind" in a foreign contract.
  2. The re-wrap block is not a general ETH handler; it is a representation converter. The constructor deliberately
  rewrites Curve's ALT_ETH_ADDRESS (0xEeee…) to ETH_ADDRESS (0x0) because "which is used by the vault internally to
  represent ETH". So when TOKEN_x == ETH_ADDRESS, the vault's own accounting already denominates that leg in native ETH,
  and TokenUtils.tokenBalance(ETH_ADDRESS) resolves to address(this).balance. The only case where the on-hand asset
  must be converted is when the vault's ASSET is WETH while the pool leg is native ETH — exactly the condition ASSET ==
  address(WETH) && TOKEN_x == ETH_ADDRESS that the code gates on. For a [ETH, USDC] pool with ASSET == USDC, native ETH
  is the correct internal form of the TOKEN_1 leg; there is no mis-accounting, so the premise "raw ETH left in the
  vault" is not an invariant break, it is the intended representation.
  3. The claimed leg can't even be produced on the ordinary redemption path. With pool [ETH, USDC] and _asset == USDC,
  TOKEN_2 == _asset so _PRIMARY_INDEX = 1. A single-sided exit calls remove_liquidity_one_coin(...,
  int8(_PRIMARY_INDEX), ...) and writes only exitBalances[1] — exitBalances[0] stays zero. A non-zero ETH-leg balance
  requires isSingleSided == false, i.e. the proportional/emergency path, which is not the unprivileged user-redemption
  flow the claim describes.
  4. The heart of the claim — __initiateWithdraw passing a non-zero yieldTokenAmount for the ETH leg to
  manager.initiateWithdraw with no msg.value and no approval, and convertToAssets then valuing a phantom escrow — rests
  entirely on code that is not in the file under review. AbstractSingleSidedLP.__initiateWithdraw, the per-token
  withdraw-request-manager lookup, and the convertToAssets escrow branch are all unshown. The one manager reference
  visible here is a single IWithdrawRequestManager _withdrawRequestManager constructor arg on the vault (not a per-token
  map), and there is no evidence in the shown code that a native-ETH leg is ever routed to a withdraw request manager
  at all — withdraw-request managers exist for tokens with redemption queues (LSTs, etc.), and a pool's raw ETH leg with
  a USDC asset has no such manager. I cannot point to the exact line that turns leftover ETH into a recorded, valued
  withdraw request, because that line is not shown.
  5. Finally, the whole scenario is deployment-shaped: it requires governance to deploy a vault whose ASSET is a
  non-WETH token against a pool with a native-ETH leg and to wire a withdraw request manager onto that ETH leg. That is
  a trusted deployment configuration choice, not something an unprivileged external actor can bring about.

  Points 2, 3, and 4 each independently defeat the claim; the tie-break rule (unshown __initiateWithdraw /
  convertToAssets, ambiguous path) settles it.

